{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18728 - PROTEIN:6326",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Getiacaulein may interact with Phosphodiesterase 6C through a series of intermediate targets, all of which are involved in the regulation of intracellular signaling and metabolic processes. The primary target of Getiacaulein is NQO1 protein, which is known to be a key player in DNA repair and antioxidant pathways. NQO1 is also a target of two other compounds, 5,7-Dihydroxy-2-(4-Hydroxy-3-Methoxyphenyl)-3-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-Trihydroxy-6-[[(3R,4R,5R,6S)-3,4,5-Trihydroxy-6-Methyloxan-2-Yl]Oxymethyl]Oxan-2-Yl]Oxychromen-4-One and 5-Hydroxy-3-Amino-2-Aceto-1,4-Naphthoquinone, both of which are implicated in modulating enzyme activity and cellular responses. These compounds are likely to act through similar mechanisms, possibly by inhibiting or activating Phosphodiesterase 6C, thereby influencing cyclic AMP levels and downstream signaling pathways. The integration of these targets highlights a potential role for Getiacaulein in modulating enzyme activity through NQO1, which may be a key mediator in its interaction with Phosphodiesterase 6C.",
